00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.079
The Dolly Zoom is one of the most disorienting and flashy camera techniques of all time

00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:09.780
And it shows up in some of the most beloved scenes in cinematic history.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:12.540
It's famous for its bizarre look but the shot also teaches us a lot about filmmaking.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:16.890
It shows the different kind of lenses and how to use them, it enhances the emotion of a scene and

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:20.540
It can be used in subtle places you may not have even noticed. Let's see how.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:26.420
The Dolly Zoom, or sometimes called 'The Vertigo Effect', started in Hitchcock's film Vertigo in 1958.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:30.780
You need a smooth track and steady zoom to pull it off nicely, which is why it wasn't developed sooner.

00:00:30.849 --> 00:00:36.599
The Shot is essentially an optical illusion caused by zooming in or out on the camera lens while tracking the camera

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:42.180
forwards or backwards. The result is the foreground stays in the same position while the background appears to squeeze or stretch

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:47.969
depending on which direction you go. It actually teaches an important principle of filming. Camera lens. If you ever wanted to see the difference between

00:00:48.129 --> 00:00:49.090
wide-angle and telephoto

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.899
lenses, a simplified example would be to observe a dolly zoom.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.360
Compare how it begins to how it ends, the dolly zoom shot can start like a telephoto lens, where the camera is far away and

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.689
the focal length is bigger There's less background in sight Because the lens concentrates

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:04.680
on a smaller portion of it. But when you change focal lens by zooming out while

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:11.069
also pushing the camera forward, you can observe more of the background while keeping the foreground in the same place. The result is filming the

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.460
same thing but the camera

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:18.239
expands to see more of the background. The change in focal length takes in a wider area so you go from a telephoto shot to

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.240
A wide-angle shot. You generally use

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:24.689
telephoto in close-ups to keep attention on the foreground. When you move the camera around while using a

00:01:24.689 --> 00:01:31.409
telephoto lens, the background whips quickly behind the foreground because the camera takes in less of the background. It makes for some pretty cool shots.

00:01:31.409 --> 00:01:31.799
People

00:01:31.799 --> 00:01:35.938
also tend to look better in telephoto because their faces aren't stretched by the wider lens

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:42.029
wide-angle takes in more of the background, so it's better for outdoor shots when you want to capture the Landscape. So each lens is a

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.380
trade-off. The dolly-zoom

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:48.479
captures the transition from one kind of lens shot to the other and it leads to different emotional responses for the viewer.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:54.779
Vertigo uses a dolly zoom to give a sense of height. This increase in height perfectly embodies how the telephoto has a more narrow view

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:55.799
of the background, so

00:01:55.799 --> 00:01:59.489
we go from capturing a lot of the background to capturing very little. The result is a dizzying

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:06.990
optical illusion. The shot is unsettling, even a little bit nauseating for some, because it's physically impossible to experience without some kind of lens

00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:13.288
alteration. In Goodfellas, the diner scene becomes all the more confusing as the landscape shifts around them in impossible ways, just as

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:17.910
Henry's world is crushing and his paranoia grows, the impossible movement of the diner shows just how

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:22.710
disoriented he feels. The zoom makes the world literally close in around him as getting caught seems almost

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.219
inevitable. As I mentioned before, the wide-angle lens makes people look A bit off.

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:31.019
With the famous dolly zoom in Jaws, the shot shifts to a wide-angle look, which makes his face look

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:37.559
just a bit more stretched and unnatural, and it unconsciously adds to the rest of the freakiness of the shot. Those three examples, Vertigo, Jaws

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:40.660
and Goodfellas are the Go-To

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:45.149
examples Whenever Anybody Mentions the Dolly Zoom. The problem is that when teaching the shot, the examples usually stop there which is a shame, because

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:51.420
it makes the shot seem like a one-off effect for legendary movies. But it's actually a lot more versatile in common than that. Its out-of-this-world

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:53.699
visual look makes it  commonly used for the

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:59.699
supernatural, or the feeling of a drug trip. I particularly enjoy its use in The Lord of the Rings, because the widening of the holes

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:05.459
in the trees gives the visual feeling that the forest is opening up a portal for the enemy to come. It's almost always used at a

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.969
movie's most climactic epic moment. So if your hero fails, it adds to the punchline.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.320
Shaun: Sorry, we're closed!

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:14.560
The shot has even found its way into animation.

00:03:14.709 --> 00:03:20.399
Brad Bird uses it extensively in his films. The food critic flashback gets enhanced with a dolly zoom effect to simulate the

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:22.300
transportation from one world to another.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:29.279
Animation, particularly computer-generated, has a distinct opportunity to use the photo in interesting ways because of animation's complete control of

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:33.719
object placement. As Remy relates to Chef Gusto the dolly zoom is no longer used to

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.689
disorient the viewer, but to show them get physically closer together,

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:41.519
indicating a relationship is born. The Incredibles has a seemingly impossible dolly scene as Mrs

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:45.450
Incredible gets closer to the temptation. Both instances are non-disruptive, but

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:50.489
also draw our attention to certain parts of the shot with its trademark squeezing effect. The dolly zoom is certainly a

00:03:50.709 --> 00:03:56.069
fantastical shot at the disposal of the filmmaker, but remember it's more than just a cheap trick. Alfred Hitchcock used it for depth,

00:03:56.260 --> 00:04:01.889
Scorsese used it to show paranoia, and Brad Bird used it for visual cues. So be on the lookout for it. Thanks for Watching

